window.HOLISTICRX_DATA = {"note":"Transcribed from Nourhan Shams's copy of The Holistic Rx: Your Guide to Healing Chronic Inflammation and Disease (Madiha Saeed, MD; Rowman & Littlefield, 2021) — the foundations chapters on inflammation, digestive health and detoxification, the Four Big S's, and the per-condition Holistic Rx protocols from its cardiology through urology chapters. Every recommendation here is the book's; none of it was written for this tool, and none of it is peer reviewed. It sits alongside the Master Compendium under each condition's From your notes section, credited separately.","source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD","chapters":[],"topics":[{"name":"Graves' disease","blurb":"Autoimmune hyperthyroidism from overproduction of thyroid hormone."},{"name":"Hashimoto's thyroiditis","blurb":"Autoimmune thyroiditis and the most common cause of hypothyroidism."},{"name":"Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)","blurb":"Chronic autoimmune disease that can attack skin, joints, brain, heart, lungs and blood cells."},{"name":"Hyperlipidemia","blurb":"Elevated cholesterol/triglycerides — a major modifiable cardiovascular risk factor addressed with diet, targeted supplements, and lab-guided therapy."},{"name":"Tooth decay","blurb":"Dental caries and enamel demineralization driven by mineral and fat-soluble-vitamin deficiency, excess sugar, and phytic acid — addressed with a remineralizing diet, targeted supplements, and homeopathy."},{"name":"Toothache","blurb":"Acute tooth or gum pain, matched here to individual homeopathic remedies by its exact character, location, and triggers."}],"sections":[{"id":"hrx_autoimmune_general_dd","chapter":"autoimmune","title":"Autoimmune conditions — the Holistic Rx (diet, detox and lifestyle)","body":["Autoimmune disease is the immune system's misguided attack on the body: excess inflammation confuses its ability to tell self from nonself, autoantibodies form against normal cells, and regulatory T cells fail to keep the response in check. There are more than eighty known autoimmune diseases, and having one makes you three times more likely to develop another.","Diet and detox special considerations: avoid nightshades (tomatoes, eggplant, potatoes, peppers), legumes/lentils, eggs, caffeine, nuts, and seeds — common food sensitivities in autoimmune disease. Fix deficiencies in calcium, iron, vitamin D, zinc, B6, B12, and folate. Eat vegetables at every meal, and favor sulfur-rich foods (cabbage, onions, garlic, leeks, cauliflower, asparagus) to help reduce symptoms; use coconut oil and olive oil for healthy fats. Raw, unprocessed honey can also boost immunity.","For thyroid autoimmunity specifically, increase fatty acids, iodine (seaweed, saltwater fish, or a multivitamin), tyrosine (red meat, chicken, seaweed, seafood), selenium (red meats, liver, chicken, turkey, fish, shellfish), zinc (red meats, chicken, seafood, seaweed), iron, omega-3s, vitamin D3, B vitamins (leafy greens, broccoli, beets, red meat, liver), and vitamin A (orange fruits/veggies, liver, kale). Kale and broccoli are goitrogens, but their benefits outweigh that concern. Avoid IV detoxification in all cases of autoimmunity.","The Four Big S's: stress management is key to healing autoimmune disease as with any chronic condition, including choosing gentler exercise — jogging and heavy weightlifting can be too harsh on the joints. Flexibility, strengthening, aerobic exercise, and body-awareness practices like tai chi, yoga, and Pilates improve posture, coordination, balance, and joint/muscular mobility. An exercise physiologist can help find what works best for you."],"conditions":["Autoimmune conditions"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_autoimmune_infections_ldn","chapter":"autoimmune","title":"Autoimmune conditions — infections and low-dose naltrexone","body":["If symptoms — especially fatigue and brain symptoms — persist after three months of the foundational GutRx, consider that an infection may be contributing. A functional medicine practitioner can test for the microbe associated with your specific condition; leaky gut, stress, and inflammatory food can reactivate dormant infections and trigger the immune system. Look for coinfections (parasites, Candida, bacterial overgrowth) alongside the primary infection, and expect treatment to take time, especially with more than one infecting agent.","Table 5.1 (Autoimmunity and Infections) pairs specific autoimmune diseases with implicated microbes: autoimmunity in general with E. coli, Proteus, Klebsiella; ankylosing spondylitis with Klebsiella; Graves' disease and Hashimoto's thyroiditis with herpes, EBV, MAP, H. pylori, Yersinia, and Campylobacter; Guillain-Barré syndrome with Campylobacter, cytomegalovirus, and EBV; multiple sclerosis with Chlamydia pneumoniae, EBV, and measles; rheumatoid arthritis with Citrobacter, Klebsiella, Proteus, Porphyromonas, EBV, E. coli, hepatitis C, and mycobacterium; rheumatic fever with Streptococcus pyogenes; and SLE/lupus, autism, uveitis, keratitis, autoimmune hepatitis, polymyositis, psoriasis, ITP, IgA nephropathy, glomerulonephritis, pemphigus, Sjögren's syndrome, type 1 diabetes mellitus, and myasthenia gravis with various combinations of EBV, cytomegalovirus, herpes 1/2/6, measles, coxsackievirus, and hepatitis C.","Viral infections: chronic viral infections (herpes 1, 2, 6; Epstein-Barr virus) can hide dormant and trigger inflammation. For EBV activity, check EBV EA-D IgG (active/reactivated infection), EBV VCA IgG/IgM (past or reactivated infection), and EBV EBNA-1 IgG (past infection, or active replication in B cells when combined with a positive VCA). Treat with Lauricidin (1 tsp three times daily, an acid derived from coconut oil) and humic acid (2 pills/day) to lower viral load; L-lysine 500 mg for prevention, 1–4 g/day with food during an outbreak. Herbal options: Lomatium (can cause a one-time rash), Cordyceps (750 mg, two capsules, three times daily for ninety days), or olive leaf extract (one capsule twice daily for sixty days). Add NAC (1,800 mg/day), thymic protein A, adaptogens, vitamin D, vitamin C (500–3,000 mg/day), or lysine for additional support.","Bacterial infections: implicated bacteria include Yersinia enterocolitica (specialized stool test), H. pylori (breath, stool antigen, or blood test), SIBO (breath test), Chlamydia pneumoniae, and Bartonella or Mycobacterium avium subspecies paratuberculosis (MAP). Treatment combines antibiotics, probiotics (dosed apart from the antibiotic), caprylic acid, and an enzyme blend of cellulase, hemicellulase, xylanase, and beta-glucanase to prevent yeast overgrowth. H. pylori: zinc, berberine, bismuth citrate, mastic gum, oil pulling, and cranberry juice. Yersinia: sweet wormwood, grapefruit seed extract, berberine, black walnut, uva ursi, or doxycycline. Garlic, licorice, quercetin, CoQ10, turmeric, and oil of oregano help broadly. SIBO: rifaximin (Xifaxan), adding neomycin or tinidazole if methane-predominant. Chlamydia pneumoniae: minocycline 100 mg twice daily for three weeks.","Lyme disease: if symptoms don't improve, consider Lyme disease (Borrelia burgdorferi), which can mimic autoimmune symptoms and affect multiple organs. Ask for advanced testing (iSpot Lyme, ELISA, western blot, Lyme PCR); antibiotic treatment may follow a positive result. Supportive foods/herbs: thyme, lemon balm, zinc, licorice root, L-lysine, lomatium root, reishi mushroom, silver hydrosol, astaxanthin, and Nascent iodine.","Fungal infections: systemic Candida is common in autoimmune disease — test with blood work and stool IgG/IgM. If positive: Diflucan 100 mg twice daily for three weeks, or nystatin 500,000 units, two capsules, three times daily for thirty to ninety days.","Parasitic infections: test for toxoplasmosis (blood) and Blastocystis hominis (stool). Treat blastocystis with Alinia 1,000 mg twice daily for three days, repeated after two weeks and again two weeks later, or nystatin as above (for fungal overgrowth alongside or after Alinia). Herbal options: CandiBactin (two capsules three times daily for sixty days), a wormwood-containing antiparasitic (600 mg twice daily for seven days, repeated in two weeks — avoid with a history of liver problems), or oil of oregano (150 mg, two capsules, three times daily for sixty days) combined with Saccharomyces boulardii (5–15 billion CFU, two to four times daily for sixty days).","Low-dose naltrexone: as an opioid antagonist, naltrexone can enhance immune function — increasing endogenous adrenaline production, reducing inflammation, and increasing regulatory T-cell cytokines including IL-1 and TGF-B — helpful for those with multiple autoimmune conditions or symptoms and very high antibodies. For autoimmunity, use only low doses, 1.5–4.5 mg/day, starting at 1.5 mg at night. It should not be a first line of defense — use it only after implementing the foundational recommendations and if symptoms persist."],"conditions":["Autoimmune conditions"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_autoimmune_celiac","chapter":"autoimmune","title":"Celiac disease — the Holistic Rx","body":["Celiac disease is an autoimmune reaction to gluten. Typical symptoms: recurrent bloating, cramping, abdominal pain, brain fog, weight changes, insomnia and other sleep issues, fatigue, headaches, delayed puberty, pale foul-smelling stool, peripheral neuropathy, joint pain, mood changes, thinning hair, pale mouth sores, tingling of hands/feet, vomiting, unexplained weight loss, unexplained infertility or recurrent miscarriage, and dermatitis herpetiformis (skin rash). Testing: IgA and IgG anti-gliadin antibodies, IgA anti-endomysial antibodies, tissue transglutaminase antibodies, total IgA, and genetic testing (HLA DQ2, HLA DQ8).","Supplements: supplements that heal leaky gut are key — digestive enzymes with amylase (a pancreatic enzyme, to enhance digestion), probiotics, B vitamins (B2, B3, B6, B9, B12), calcium, magnesium, zinc, and other gut-healing agents like L-glutamine, N-acetyl-D-glucosamine, and gamma oryzanol. In children, start with digestive enzymes and probiotics.","Homeopathy: early constitutional treatment from an experienced homeopath — matched to the person's most distressing symptoms — can help with celiac-related constipation, increased frequency of bulky/offensive-smelling stools, allergies, gastroenteritis, and emotional issues.","Acupressure: P6, SP6, CV12.","Aromatherapy: caraway, lavender, and bitter orange help harmonize gut bacterial balance. Peppermint oil and cumin can ease digestive symptoms; ginger and turmeric protect the stomach and support digestion. Peppermint and ginger work well together for nausea."],"conditions":["Celiac disease"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_autoimmune_graves","chapter":"autoimmune","title":"Graves' disease — the Holistic Rx","body":["Graves' disease is an autoimmune disorder causing overproduction of thyroid hormone. Symptoms: mood changes, racing heart, muscle pain, insomnia, digestive issues, hand/finger tremors, increased perspiration or warm moist skin, thyroid gland enlargement, irregular periods, skin-texture changes (thickened skin on the lower legs or red bumps — pretibial myxedema), and eye problems (bulging/exophthalmos, pain, redness, vision loss). Tests: TSH, free T4, free T3, thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulins (TSI), and TSH receptor antibody.","Supplements: B-complex (50 mg twice daily). L-carnitine (start 2,000 mg/day, work up to 4,000 mg/day as needed) can reduce heart palpitations, tremors, insomnia, and anxiety. CoQ10 (100–400 mg/day with a fat-containing meal) helps muscle weakness and pain. Plant sterols to balance the immune system. Selenium (200 mcg/day) may slow progression of eye symptoms. Magnesium (400 mg/day) helps prevent arrhythmias. Calcium citrate 1,000 mg (ages 19–50) or 1,200 mg (women 51+ and men over 71), alongside optimized vitamin D. Bugleweed (start 2 mL three times daily, increasing to 4 mL after three days and 6 mL three days later) can prevent thyroid damage and calm the thyroid, keeping antibodies from binding to it. Motherwort liquid extract (start 2 mL daily, increase to 4 mL after five days) helps palpitations, sleep issues, appetite issues, and anxiety — talk to your doctor, and don't combine with sedating medications. Lemon balm (start 300 mg, increase to 600 mg after seven days) inhibits antibodies from binding to the thyroid gland. Glucomannan (start 1.5 g twice daily, work up to 3.0 g twice daily) decreases circulating thyroid hormone levels.","Homeopathy: Iodum 30C for someone who feels very hot, is always in a hurry, obsessive, dark-haired, and dark-eyed — also indicated for weight loss despite an increased appetite, extreme heat sensitivity, and seeking coolness. Natrum mur 30C for palpitations and constipation, in someone who looks anemic, is weak, and craves salt. Lycopus 30C for a racing, pounding heart from nervous irritation, with exophthalmos. Sulphur for heat intolerance. Pulsatilla for heat intolerance with no thirst. Lachesis for hot flashes with intolerance of tight clothing. Lycopodium for weight loss (more upper body), craving warm drinks and sweets. Phosphorus for extreme weakness with excessive, offensive stool. Others that can be used: Kali iodatum, Ferrum metallicum, Lycopodium clavatum, and Spongia tosta.","Acupressure: GV17, ST9, P6, HT7, LI4, ST30.","Aromatherapy: frankincense (two drops daily on the roof of the mouth) and myrrh (applied over the thyroid area twice daily) can help, along with anti-inflammatory oils like lavender, rose, and tea tree — combined with coconut oil, these can soothe irritated skin."],"conditions":["Graves' disease"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_autoimmune_hashimotos","chapter":"autoimmune","title":"Hashimoto's thyroiditis — the Holistic Rx","body":["Hashimoto's is autoimmune thyroiditis. Symptoms: fatigue, depression and mood disorders, weight gain, feeling cold easily, digestive issues, muscular pain, joint stiffness and swelling, hair loss, frequent urination and excessive thirst, and menstrual changes. Assessment: TSH, free T4, free T3, antithyroglobulin antibody, and antithyroid peroxidase antibodies (which can appear decades before TSH is affected), plus reverse T3 and ferritin; vitamin A, selenium, and zinc can also be checked. Landmarks of healing: symptom improvement or resolution, antibody count dropping first under 100 and then under 35, and regeneration of thyroid tissue.","Supplements: probiotics. Multivitamin with vitamin A (5,000–10,000 IU/day). Vitamin E (400 IU). B vitamins: riboflavin (50 mg/day), niacin (200 mg/day), B6 (50 mg/day), folate (800 mcg/day), B12 (1,000 mcg/day of methylcobalamin), biotin (5,000 mcg/day), and B1 (600 mg/day specifically for fatigue). Iodine (150–300 mcg/day). Zinc (25–50 mg/day). Magnesium (500 mg/day). Vitamin C (1,000 mg/day). Iron glycinate (25 mg/day; keep ferritin between 50 and 100 if still menstruating). Omega-3 fish oil (1,000 mg twice daily with food). Selenium (200–400 mcg) has been shown to help regulate the hormones. Others: Antabine, shown to reduce TPO antibodies by preventing the inflammatory immune system from responding; also curcumin, Thytrophin PMG, or Drenatrophin PMG. Children with hypothyroidism: iodine (50–900 mcg/day), selenium (20–200 mcg/day), tyrosine (500 mg/day), vitamin A (200,000 IU as one dose), and seaweed/kelp (2 tbsp/day).","Homeopathy: Arsenicum 30C every twelve hours for up to five days while a constitutional remedy is being found. Calcarea carbonica for constipation (hard-then-soft stool) and heavy menses. Sepia for weak patients with a pale yellow face, feeling cold, and a sensation of bearing-down; helps control excessive hair loss — these patients crave acidic foods and pickles. Lycopodium for gastric problems with excessive flatulence, craving hot foods, weakness, and irritability. Graphites for those who tire easily, are obese, become sad when listening to music, and are constipated and timid. Nux vomica for hypothyroidism with people who are easily offended, constipated, bloated after eating, crave stimulants, and crave fatty and spicy foods; these people also feel cold.","Acupressure: ST36, P6, KD3, GV7, KD7, SP6, LI10, LI11.","Aromatherapy: frankincense can help lower thyroid antibodies when used alongside other modalities. Peppermint oil can help reduce brain fog, depression, headaches, and digestive issues. Lavender and myrtle may help restore hair and calm anxiety. Others that help hypothyroidism symptoms: lemongrass, myrrh, clove, basil, marjoram, rosemary, grapefruit, and clary sage."],"conditions":["Hashimoto's thyroiditis"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_autoimmune_ms","chapter":"autoimmune","title":"Multiple sclerosis — the Holistic Rx","body":["Multiple sclerosis (MS) occurs when the immune system attacks the myelin sheath covering nerve fibers, permanently damaging the nerves and disrupting nervous-system communication. Symptoms depend on the location and extent of the damage and may include fatigue, dizziness, slurred speech, prolonged double vision, involuntary eye movement (nystagmus), tremor, bowel and bladder problems, poor coordination, numbness or weakness in one or more limbs or the trunk (often one-sided), and electric-shock sensations with certain movements (Lhermitte's sign). Diagnosis: imaging and spinal tap.","Supplements: vitamin E (400 mg of mixed tocotrienols or mixed tocopherols daily). High-potency B-complex (B-100) daily, including B6, B7 (biotin), and B12. Cyanocobalamin (1,000 mcg sublingually each day). L-methylfolate (levomefolic acid, 1,000 mcg daily). CoQ10 (200 mg daily; doses up to 2,400 mg/day have been well tolerated, though above 600 mg/day some note heartburn, digestive upset, and headaches — it may also reduce the effectiveness of blood-thinning medications). Digestive enzymes with protease, bromelain, amylase, and lipase. Algae (two to eight capsules, or up to one teaspoon of powder, daily). Alpha lipoic acid (300–600 mg/day) for nerve pain; aged garlic to lower oxidative stress and improve blood flow.","Homeopathy: Agaricus for uncoordinated eye movement, muscle spasms, twitching, poor coordination, sharp shooting pains, and weak, shaky movements. Alumina for weakness, progressive paralysis, heaviness of the lower extremities, confusion, constipation, and dry skin or mucous membranes — better in open air and damp weather, worse in the morning and in a warm room. Argentum nitricum for progressive paralysis, poor coordination, loss of balance, trembling, always feeling in a rush, and multiple phobias. Arsenicum album for anxious, pessimistic, restless people with progressive paralysis and burning sensations. Causticum for slow, progressive paralysis of the limbs, numbness of hands and feet, problems with breathing, swallowing, and speech, incontinence, and restlessness at night. Cocculus for paralysis with dizziness when trying to focus on moving objects, painful contracture of the limbs and trunk, and feeling too weak to talk loudly. Conium for paralysis of the eye muscles and ascending paralysis beginning with weakness and heaviness in the thighs and lower limbs, worse in the morning. Gelsemium sempervirens for trembling and weak muscles, heavy eyelids, double vision, numbness of the face and tongue, improvement after urination, and dizziness/drowsiness. Ignatia amara for twitching and spasms especially when MS presents after grief, with emotional upset and heightened senses. Kali phos for weakness in the back and limbs worse with exercise, leaving pain, fatigue, and possible inability to move the extremity. Lachesis when jealousy or anger worsen symptoms, more left-sided, worse premenstrually or at the start of menopause. Natrum muriaticum for optic neuritis (eye pain and temporary vision loss) with body numbness, worse in the sun, coming on after grief, low mood, and awkwardness such as dropping things. Nux vomica for those who crave alcohol and stimulants, are irritable, highly driven, and intolerant of contradiction. Magnesia phos for painful spasms of twitching or jerking, improved by pressure, friction, and warmth. Phosphorus for vision problems and incontinence with numbness in the hands and feet, craving ice-cold drinks, exaggerated reflexes, fainting easily, and episodes linked to emotional upset. Plumbum for progressive paralysis, heaviness, and wasting of the muscles, especially in the legs. Tarentula for very jerky movements of the hands, feet, and tongue.","Acupressure: GV20 (headaches, poor memory, mental disorders, ringing in the ears, fatigue, and poor sleep); GV26, P6, LU9, GB34, LU1 (anxiety or depression); GV26 (reduces muscle spasms and relieves dizziness); ST36 (improves digestion); SP6, H7, B2, B23, B13, GB20, and LV3.","Aromatherapy: relaxing oils and those that support the neurological system are important — find what works best and rotate as needed. Frankincense and helichrysum support the neurological system; frankincense can be taken internally, two drops three times daily for three weeks, then one week off, repeating the cycle, while helichrysum can be applied to the neck or temples twice daily. Basil oil and cypress oil can reduce MS symptoms by improving circulation and muscle tone. Other good choices: lavender, jasmine, neroli, geranium, ylang-ylang, and rose."],"conditions":["Multiple sclerosis"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_autoimmune_lupus","chapter":"autoimmune","title":"Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) — the Holistic Rx","body":["Lupus is a chronic autoimmune condition in which the body attacks its own tissues, potentially affecting the skin, brain, heart, lungs, and blood cells. Presentation varies widely and may come on suddenly or develop slowly, and may be mild or severe, temporary or permanent. Common signs and symptoms: fatigue, joint pain, dry eyes, sun sensitivity, skin lesions that worsen with sun exposure, chest pain, a butterfly-shaped rash across the bridge of the nose and cheeks, fingers and toes that turn white or blue with cold or stress (Raynaud's phenomenon), headaches, confusion, and memory loss. Diagnosis is made via history, physical exam, and laboratory testing.","Supplements: plant sterols and sterolins (20 mg three times daily on an empty stomach). Methylsulfonylmethane (MSM) (2,000–8,000 mg daily) for its natural anti-inflammatory benefits — reduce the dose if diarrhea occurs. Digestive enzymes (1–2 capsules of a full-spectrum enzyme product with each meal). Green superfood supplements chlorella and spirulina are key for alkalizing and detoxifying the body. Boswellia (1,200–1,500 mg of a standardized extract containing 60–65 percent boswellic acids, two to three times daily) for its anti-inflammatory effects. DHEA to support adrenal hormones — have DHEA-S measured in the blood and dose to bring levels above 100 mcg/dl; the usual recommended dose is 25 mg, but confirm the right dose against your own DHEA-S level. Indole-3-carbinol (DIM), broccoli extracts, and sulforaphane all help detoxify estrogens.","Homeopathy: Arsenicum album for anxious, restless individuals with a burning sensation in the joints that feels better with warmth. Belladonna when pain and swelling come on suddenly, with hot, red joints and burning pain worse with motion. Sepia for women whose lupus flares near their menstrual cycle, with constipation, depression, irritability, a tendency to feel cold, and cravings for sweet, salty, and sour foods. Pulsatilla when symptoms improve with cold and pain wanders from joint to joint. Rhus toxicodendron for pain that increases with rest and is worse in cold and damp, with joint stiffness that improves with movement and warmth. Sulphur for strong cravings for spicy foods and ice-cold drinks, getting overheated easily, and burning pain relieved by cold applications.","Acupressure: S13 (stiff neck, headaches, ringing in the ears, weakness of the arms, and back muscle spasm/ache); GV26, SP10, SP4, SP6, SP21, LI4, LI11, LV3, LV5.","Aromatherapy: use essential oils that promote relaxation, help digestion, and support the neurological system — good starting choices are lemon verbena, lavender, rose, jasmine, and geranium. Black pepper and ginger encourage blood flow and help revive tired joints and muscles; for digestive issues, ginger oil can be taken internally, three drops, two to three times daily. Frankincense essential oil is effective at reducing inflammation and immune reactions, and helichrysum oil supports the nervous system. For skin inflammation, lavender and geranium oils work well; lemon balm or juniper in a hot bath can help detoxify the body and lower inflammation."],"conditions":["Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_autoimmune_ra","chapter":"autoimmune","title":"Rheumatoid arthritis — the Holistic Rx","body":["Rheumatoid arthritis (RA) occurs when inflammation attacks the lining of the joints, in addition to a wide variety of other body systems including the eyes, lungs, heart, blood vessels, and skin. Signs and symptoms: fatigue, fever, weight loss, joint stiffness (usually worse in the morning and after inactivity), and tender, warm, swollen joints — early on it can affect the smaller joints and then progress to larger joints like wrists, elbows, shoulders, knees, and hips. Diagnosis is made via history, a physical exam, and lab work.","Supplements: gamma linolenic acid (GLA) (450–500 mg/day). Curcumin and boswellia reduce pain and inflammation in the joints — can be used alone or in a combination product. Turmeric (1,000 mg once a day). Fish oil (1–3 g of EPA/DHA per day). Others: bromelain, MSM, glucosamine, vitamin C (500–1,000 mg/day), selenium (200–400 mcg/day), vitamin E (200–400 IU/day), ginger (100–200 mg three times daily), and one of grapeseed extract (100–300 mg/day), pine bark extract (100–300 mg/day), or another antioxidant. Juvenile RA: 5-MTHF (200–400 mcg/day), vitamin C (500–1,000 mg/day), calcium (1,000 mg/day), vitamin D (1,000–4,000 IU/day), CoQ10 (10–250 mg/day), vitamin E (200–400 IU/day), fish oil (2–4 g of EPA/day), zinc (10–20 mg/day), selenium (20–200 mcg/day), and castor oil applied topically to the joints daily.","Homeopathy: Bryonia for severe pain made worse by movement, the slightest touch, or heat, and relieved by cold applications. Pulsatilla for pain that passes from one part of the body to another and is made worse by warm environments; suitable if fatty food is hard to digest and there's a tendency to feel sad. Causticum for pain accompanied by muscle spasms that tends to improve in wet weather or with warmth. Rhus tox for stiffness that tends to wear off with movement (causing restlessness), worse in cold and damp weather, after rest, or in the morning. Colchicum for stiffness and pain worse in winter and during the nighttime hours. Dulcamara for stiffness and pain worse when overheated, and in cold or damp weather. Mercurius for aches and pains worsened by heat and cold, worse at night, accompanied by offensive sweat. Ruta for pain that especially affects the tendonous insertions of muscles and large joints. Rhododendron for aches and pains worse in cold weather and during electrical storms, located in the muscles and ligaments. Calcarea hypophos for sharp pains in the wrists and hands. Aconite for sudden onset of sharp pain, worse in cold and dry weather, with general restlessness and anxiety.","Acupressure: the Yang Lin Qian point (for inflammation and joint pain); LI4, LV2, LV5, GB41, GB34.","Aromatherapy: ginger essential oil has anti-inflammatory and analgesic effects; other helpful oils include juniper, vetiver, ylang-ylang, turmeric, frankincense, myrrh, and orange essential oils."],"conditions":["Rheumatoid arthritis"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_cardiology_dd_general","chapter":"cardiology","title":"Cardiology — the Holistic Rx (diet, detox and lifestyle)","body":["Almost all cardiac risk factors are related to lifestyle, environment, and inflammation, so treating insulin resistance and lowering inflammation are the cornerstones of preventing and healing heart disease. Add herbs and spices — turmeric, garlic, ginger, cinnamon, curry powder, rosemary, thyme, basil, chili peppers, and coriander — and focus on fiber and healthy fats. Green, black, and oolong tea are great for the heart, as they contain flavonoids and have an anticlotting effect. Increase fiber up to 50 g per day with nuts, seeds, berries, and vegetables. High-potassium foods (avocados, melons, spinach, broccoli, squash) and apple cider vinegar (helps keep the body alkaline) have been shown to improve blood pressure; omega-3 foods like grass-fed beef, wild-caught salmon, chia, and flax are great for the heart. Other foods to focus on: pomegranate juice (8 oz)/seeds, watercress, goji berries, apples, capers, olives, onions, green pepper, and scallions. Limit/eliminate alcohol and caffeine, and eat about an ounce of dark chocolate daily. Detoxify the environment from lead, cadmium, and mercury.","The Four Big S's: stress raises blood pressure — it constricts blood vessels and worsens heart disease — so a daily relaxation technique is important. Moderate exercise lowers insulin resistance, inflammation, and blood pressure (consult a doctor before starting a program, as some conditions need to be addressed first). Chiropractic care can also improve cardiovascular problems."],"conditions":["Atherosclerosis","Hypertension","Hyperlipidemia"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_cardiology_atherosclerosis","chapter":"cardiology","title":"Atherosclerosis / coronary heart disease — the Holistic Rx","body":["Coronary heart disease is a chronic disease in which plaque (a waxy substance) builds up over many years inside the coronary arteries that supply oxygen-rich blood to the heart. Symptoms: chest pain or pain radiating into the arm, neck, jaw, or back (may feel like indigestion), shortness of breath, sleep issues, and fatigue; pain can be triggered by stress and worsens with activity — any chest pain warrants an immediate ER visit. Once an acute event has passed, preventing another is accomplished with lifestyle changes.","Labs: micronutrient testing, carotid doppler, stress testing, coronary artery calcium scoring, and carotid intima-media thickness testing (especially once past age fifty, or if smoking, high cholesterol, or a family history of vascular disease) — plus Endo-PAT testing for those with known or suspected disease. Advanced lipid testing breaks lipoprotein particles down by size/subclass (small, dense, oxidized particles are the ones that squeeze through vessel-wall cracks and seed plaque) and also flags oxidative stress, endothelial dysfunction, and inflammation markers. A comprehensive nutrition panel (e.g., ION panel) evaluates amino acids, vitamins, minerals, antioxidants, fats, urinary organic acids (how effectively the body turns food into energy), and heavy metals — correcting deficiencies found here, alongside lifestyle change, can meaningfully lower cardiovascular risk. A lipoprotein(a) test helps detect early cardiovascular disease, especially alongside an abnormal calcium score or thickened carotid artery — always look at the whole picture rather than any single value.","Supplements: fish oil (1 tablespoon daily). B vitamins — B1, B3/niacin (500 mg two to three times daily), B6 (50 mg/day), B9, B12. Taurine (3 g twice daily). CoQ10 (100–300 mg daily) — an antioxidant that protects the heart and vessels from free-radical damage and helps stabilize blood sugar. Garlic/Allium sativum (1,200 mg/day) — antioxidant and natural blood-thinning properties; reduces cholesterol and homocysteine. Curcumin (500–1,000 mg/day). Magnesium (500 mg daily) for circulation and blood pressure. Carotenoids. Vitamin C (250–500 mg twice daily) — avoid supplemental doses over 300 mg within 24 hours of lab testing, as it can interfere with results. Vitamins D, E, and K. Carnitine tartrate (500–1,000 mg twice daily, up to 3 g twice daily) to produce ATP and power the heart. Others: quercetin (250–1,000 mg/day), choline (do not exceed 3,500 mg/day; pair with B vitamins, particularly pantothenic acid), grapeseed extract (300 mg/day), L-citrulline (500 mg/day), sytrinol (150 mg/day), ALA (300 mg twice daily), PQQ (20 mg/day), pterostilbene (50 mg/day), ribose (5 g two to three times daily — mitochondrial energy production), NAC (600 mg/day), resveratrol (250 mg/day), alpha-lipoic acid, L-theanine, green tea extract, ashwagandha, berberine (500–1,000 mg/day), selenium, red yeast rice (1,200–2,400 mg daily — lowers LDL and reduces heart-related deaths), and hawthorn (500–900 mg daily — improves circulation to the heart and lowers blood pressure). Glucomannan capsules can help bind toxins. Branched-chain amino acids (leucine, valine, isoleucine in a 4:1:1 ratio; 5,000 mg/day), phosphatidylserine (300–600 mg twice daily).","Plaque stabilization and reversal/reduction: vitamin K2 — menaquinone-7 (100–500 mcg/day) and MK-4 (1,000 mcg/day) — omega-3 (5 g/day), aged garlic extract (600–1,200 mg twice daily), curcumin (1,000–5,000 mg/day), quercetin (500 mg twice daily), vitamin C (1,000 mg twice daily).","Homeopathy: see a licensed homeopath for a constitutional remedy; the following are for temporary/emergency use only. If experiencing heart-attack symptoms, call 911 first — while waiting for the ambulance, a 30C potency can be taken every two to three minutes. Aconitum napellus for numbness and pain in the left arm with intense fear and anxiety. Arnica for a squeezing or bruising sensation in the heart. Cactus for angina, especially with the sensation of a band tightening around the chest.","Acupressure: H3, H4, H7 (stimulates circulation); GV24.5 (produces a sense of calm); CV17 (reduces sadness/anxiety); P3 (eases pain); P6 (improves anxiety-related palpitations); SP4; B15. For chest pain: HP4, HP7, HP6, K27, CV14, CV17.","Aromatherapy: essential oils help inflammation and reduce oxidative stress — ylang ylang, lemon, frankincense, lemongrass, ginger, and helichrysum. For poor circulation, a stimulating massage blend of almond oil with thyme, black pepper, ginger, rosemary, and clove essential oils."],"conditions":["Atherosclerosis"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_cardiology_hyperlipidemia","chapter":"cardiology","title":"Hyperlipidemia — the Holistic Rx","body":["One in three Americans has elevated cholesterol. Statins block HMG-CoA reductase, the liver enzyme that drives cholesterol production, thereby lowering blood cholesterol — but among people who take a statin for five years, only about one in eighty-three lives is saved, one in one hundred develops diabetes, one in ten suffers muscular damage, and roughly 18 percent develop undesirable side effects (liver dysfunction, memory loss, increased cancer risk, anemia, sexual dysfunction). Statins also block CoQ10, an antioxidant enzyme central to cellular energy production, heart function, and blood-glucose regulation. Some people do benefit from a statin — always discuss risks versus benefits with your doctor, as alternatives exist depending on your situation.","Supplements: fish oil (1–2 g EPA/DHA per day) to reduce cholesterol and triglycerides. CoQ10 (200–300 mg daily), especially if on lipid-lowering medication. Red yeast rice (start 600 mg twice daily, up to 1,200 mg twice daily at bedtime; take 25–100 mg CoQ10 alongside to prevent deficiency; look for a citrinin-free product). Niacin (1,500–3,000 mg/day if diabetic — high doses can raise blood sugar). B5/pantothenic acid. Chromium. Choline. Phytosterols/plant sterols and stanols (2,000 mg/day). Others: garlic (500 mg/day) lowers cholesterol and raises HDL; berberine (500 mg two to three times daily); guggul (1,500 mg/day standardized to 5% guggulsterones, equivalent to 75 mg guggulsterones — raises HDL, lowers cholesterol); glucomannan (two to four capsules fifteen minutes before meals with a glass of water); artichoke extract (500 mg three times daily); acetyl-L-carnitine (1,000 mg daily) for high triglycerides.","Children: fish oil (EPA/DHA 1–2 g daily), pycnogenol (50–100 mg daily), vitamin B3 (5–20 mg daily), decaf green tea (4–6 cups daily), psyllium (6 g daily), plant sterols (2–2.5 g daily).","Homeopathy: Allium sativum for excessive meat-eaters with belching, burning, and increased appetite. Aurum metallicum for a rapid pulse, depression, or anger, with pain behind the sternum especially at night. Baryta muriaticum for elderly patients with vertigo and heaviness. Nux vomica for those who crave alcohol and fatty foods. Calcarea carbonica for those who are overweight, cold-sensitive, with continuous scalp perspiration and tight, palpitating chest. Lycopodium for a desire for sweets with flatulence and other digestive symptoms.","Acupressure: Liver point, LI11, LI4, and LV3.","Aromatherapy: essential oils can lower cholesterol by decreasing emotional stress and improving circulation, with cardio-supportive and antioxidant properties — lavender, cypress, and rosemary oil support overall heart health."],"conditions":["Hyperlipidemia"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_cardiology_hypertension","chapter":"cardiology","title":"Hypertension — the Holistic Rx","body":["Hypertension (high blood pressure) reflects resistance to blood flow in the arteries and the volume of blood the heart pumps. Most people are asymptomatic, though some have headaches, nosebleeds, or shortness of breath with severe hypertension. Diagnosis is made by history and office blood-pressure measurement.","Supplements: if already on BP medication, talk to your doctor first — these can lower pressure further and combining can be dangerous. Omega-3 fatty acids (1,000–2,000 mg daily EPA/DHA) reduce blood pressure with long-term use. Magnesium (500 mg daily) relaxes smooth muscle. CoQ10 (100–300 mg daily) reduces blood pressure and supports heart health — if on warfarin, monitor levels, as it can lower them (GI side effects can occur). Garlic (600 mg aged extract). Others: vitamin C (600 mg daily) induces sodium diuresis; L-arginine (4–8 g/day) or L-citrulline (2–4 g/day); hawthorn/Crataegus oxyacantha (250 mg standardized extract three times daily) dilates artery walls and lowers blood pressure; hibiscus tea (1.25 g steeped in 240 mL water, three times daily). If on diuretics: calcium (750 mg), potassium (100 mg), vitamin B1 (300 mg), P5P/B6 (50 mg), zinc (25 mg), magnesium glycinate (240 mg/day). If on a beta blocker: CoQ10 (start 200–300 mg/day) and melatonin (6 mg at bedtime).","Children: fish oil (EPA/DHA 1–2 g daily), magnesium (200–400 mg daily), arginine (1–2 g daily).","Homeopathy (hypertension and atherosclerosis): Arsenicum album for those who are pale, thin, and cold-sensitive with alternating weakness and vascular lesions; also (15C) for lipid disorders. Arsenicum iodatum to help prevent arterial sclerosis, preventive or curative (five pellets daily to once weekly). Baryta carbonica for elderly patients with high blood pressure, memory disorders, headaches, fatigue, palpitations, and possible aneurysm. Sulphur for hyperphagic, congestive presentations. Thuja occidentalis for truncal obesity. Natrum sulphuricum 15C for heaviness of trunk/shoulders/hips with a tendency to joint pain, bronchitis, and frequent diarrhea. Natrum muriaticum for high blood pressure after emotional upset, salt cravings, intense thirst, sun intolerance, desire to be alone, headaches, palpitations, and insomnia. Phosphorus for labile hypertension with salt cravings, nervousness, and frequent fainting spells. Vanadium for frequent fainting, dizziness, a sense of chest compression, confusion, and mental deterioration. Glonoinum for a pounding arterial sensation with tachycardia, congestion, a tight headache, flushed face, and throbbing carotid pain worse with heat, sun, or alcohol. Arnica to limit hemorrhage/edema and prevent thrombosis via its action on capillaries. Arsenicum metallicum (9C daily) for anxious, depressive patients with hypertensive vascular erethism, palpitations, extrasystoles, and hot flashes that improve with hot air. Aconitum for acute hypertensive attacks with a congested face, tense pulse, severe headache, palpitations, anxiety, and agitation. Argentum nitricum for stress-triggered symptoms with cravings for salt and sweets. Belladonna for a flushed face, pounding headaches, and immediate heat with cold hands/feet and light sensitivity. Lachesis for suspicious, jealous, talkative patients who feel warm and congested, are intolerant of anything touching the neck, bruise easily, and are prone to hypertension around menopause that improves with bleeding (menses, nosebleed). Nux vomica for stress-driven hypertension with irritability, impatience, stimulant/alcohol cravings, overeating, digestive issues, chilliness, and constipation, improving after a short nap. Ignatia for sudden BP spikes with hypersensitivity to worry and frustration. Calcarea fluorica for early-onset atherosclerosis with ligament hyperlaxity. Lycopodium for heart disease with cholesterol, liver, uric-acid, or kidney issues. Plumbum metallicum for renal insufficiency and thin arteries, especially in the elderly. Silicea for thin patients prone to osteoporosis and chronic suppurations. Calcarea carbonica (15C) for familial obesity with a tendency to diabetes, polyps, and kidney/gallbladder stones.","Acupressure: ST36, H7, LI11, P6, LV2 (acute stage), LV3 (during remission); GB20, K3, B38, CV17 (chest tension with stress); GV20, LI4, K1, GB34.","Aromatherapy: essential oils decrease emotional and oxidative stress while dilating arteries — lavender, ylang-ylang, frankincense, geranium, mandarin, marjoram, eucalyptus citriodora, yarrow, and clary sage calm the body and lower blood pressure. A juniper oil massage helps break down fatty deposits in the body and arteries. For atherosclerosis specifically: lemon, ginger, black pepper, basil, linalool, rosemary, cineole, and juniper."],"conditions":["Hypertension"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_dental_general_dd","chapter":"dental","title":"Dental health — the Holistic Rx (diet, detox and lifestyle)","body":["Healing the gut, keeping blood sugar regulated, and creating an alkaline environment is key in healing tooth decay and getting you on the path to remineralization. Four things can contribute to tooth decay: lack of minerals in the diet, like magnesium, calcium, and phosphorus; lack of fat-soluble vitamins like D, A, E, and K; too much sugar consumption; and too much phytic-acid-rich foods. Limit foods high in phytic acid — grains, nuts, seeds, and legumes — especially when starting out the process of remineralization, and incorporate soups with gut-healing bone broth daily.","Eat high amounts of fat-soluble vitamins and healthy fats from fermented cod liver oil, grass-fed organic raw dairy (if tolerated, eat butter), seafood, and coconut oil. Focus on bone broths and organ and gland meats like bone marrow and liver to help boost the endocrine system and balance hormones. Monitor sweet intake, including maple syrup, fruit, and honey. Activator X — important for tooth mineralization and bright, healthy teeth — is found primarily in grass-fed butter and raw milk; aim for 1–1½ tablespoons per day of grass-fed butter if tolerated. Other foods to incorporate for healthy teeth: zucchini, string beans, kale, chard, seaweed, vegetable juice, and fermented foods. Cranberry juice has anti-adhesion properties and can decrease bad bacteria in the mouth.","Add a remineralizing or special probiotic toothpaste and practice oil pulling to support tooth and gum health. To help prevent gum disease, aim the toothbrush at the gum line while brushing. Charcoal can help whiten teeth. To further optimize oral health, use colloidal silver (swish and swallow, one teaspoon, three times per day for sixty seconds) and spray xylitol on the gums three times per day.","The Four Big S's: lowering stress and getting enough sleep have a large impact on oral health — they help stabilize hormones and optimize calcium and other nutrient levels in the body. Practicing gratitude daily will keep you smiling."],"conditions":["Gingivitis","Tooth decay"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_dental_tooth_decay","chapter":"dental","title":"Tooth decay — the Holistic Rx (supplements and homeopathy)","body":["Supplements: fermented cod liver oil (½–1½ tsp/day total), gelatin, B vitamins like B6, B9, and B12, CoQ10, oral probiotics, calcium, magnesium, vitamin C, vitamin D, vitamin K, omega-3, and zinc. For added fat-soluble nutrients, add skate liver oil (⅛–¼ tsp, two to three times per day), or a butter-and-fermented-cod-liver-oil mixture. Children's dosing (butter/cod liver oil mixture): 25 lbs — ¼ tsp/day; 35 lbs — ⅓ tsp/day; 45 lbs — ½ tsp/day; 55 lbs — ⅔ tsp/day.","Homeopathy for tooth decay: Mercurius for decay that is black and loose with bleeding gums and excessive salivation. Silicea for multiple decayed teeth, especially in thin children with a large head. Calcarea for those who are overweight, prone to head sweats, with decay in many teeth. Kreosotum for black tooth decay with foul-smelling breath and constipation. Sulphur for tooth decay with sensitivity to touch; teeth are black and hurt after food or drink."],"conditions":["Tooth decay"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_dental_toothache_homeopathy","chapter":"dental","title":"Toothache — the Holistic Rx (homeopathy)","body":["Plantago for tooth pain that improves with eating and worsens with cold air or pressure; mouth full of saliva, pain feels nervy. Coffea for pain improved with cold and worse with heat. Chamomilla for unbearable pain with a sore head, worsened with food, drink, coffee, at night, and by cold air.","Mercurius for tooth pain that shoots up to the ears, with loose teeth, tender gums that bleed easily, bad breath, and marked thirst. Staphisagria for tearing toothache with red cheeks and a disintegrating black tooth, worse with pressure, food, and cold air. Pulsatilla for tooth pain improved in open air and cold water, worsened with hot food or drink, in a person who isn't thirsty.","Arnica for pain after a filling or an extraction. Belladonna for a developing gum boil that leads to throbbing pain and a dry mouth. Apis for burning, stinging toothache with swollen, tight-feeling gums.","Bryonia for teeth that feel too long, with pain improved by lying on the painful side and by pressure, worse with hot food, drink, and movement. Calcarea for toothache worse with food, cold drinks, or during pregnancy. Magnesia phosphorica for electric-shock-type pain that is soothed by warmth."],"conditions":["Toothache"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_dental_acupressure_aromatherapy","chapter":"dental","title":"Dental health — the Holistic Rx (acupressure and aromatherapy)","body":["Acupressure: LI4, ST6, ST7, ST36, TW13, ST44, K3, SJ2, SJ5, GV14, SI18. Lower jaw: ST6, ST5, TB17. Molars: TB23, GB2. Distal points: LI1, LI4, ST40, B60.","Aromatherapy: essential oils can reduce gum inflammation — myrrh and clove have a numbing effect that helps relieve pain and soreness. Oregano is antibacterial and can reduce bad breath. German chamomile and lemon can also be used for a toothache. Others good for mouth and gum care are cistus, cardamom, fennel, tea tree, peppermint, and nutmeg. Add peppermint oil along with tea tree oil to your toothbrush for natural whitening."],"conditions":["Gingivitis","Tooth decay","Toothache"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"}]};